  "vulnerability_information": "# HSTS cache import silently drops a longer-lived child beneath an includeSubDomains parent\n\n## Summary\n\nLoading an HSTS cache (file or `CURLOPT_HSTSREADFUNCTION` callback) can silently discard a child entry. When a live `includeSubDomains` parent is imported before a longer-lived `includeSubDomains` child, only the parent is retained. After the parent expires, the child's independently valid HSTS policy is gone and curl sends a plaintext HTTP request instead of upgrading to HTTPS. This violates the independent-policy model required by RFC 6797 §5.3.\n\n## Affected versions\n\nThe file-load path regressed in commit `a94973805df96269bf3f3bf0a20ccb9887313316` (CVE-2024-9681 fix), first released in curl 8.11.0. The callback-load path inherited the same logic in commit `e65ba1bd349a7affb09275a442b6a0837df079d9`, released in curl 8.20.0. Verified at `c2676bf9e639e31e44b12a2cc82cf2046231b056`.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`hsts_add_host_expire()` in `lib/hsts.c` calls `hsts_check(h, host, hostlen, subdomain)` with `subdomain=TRUE` at line 464. `hsts_check()` performs hierarchical matching: if the parent `.example.test` is already loaded with `includeSubDomains`, it returns the parent as a match for `child.example.test`. The caller creates an entry only when no match exists, and its update branch runs only when the returned hostname exactly matches the imported hostname. An ancestor match therefore causes neither an update nor a creation — the child is silently dropped.\n\nNetwork-learned `Strict-Transport-Security` processing does not have this problem because `Curl_hsts_parse()` uses exact-only lookup (`hsts_check(..., FALSE)`) at line 296.\n\n## Reproduction steps\nBuild curl from current master (or any version since 8.11.0) with HSTS support enabled and ENABLE_DEBUG=ON (for CURL_TIME clock control).\nCreate an HSTS cache file with two includeSubDomains entries where the parent is listed first and the child has a later expiry, e.g.:\n.example.test \"20241010 00:00:00\"\n.child.example.test \"20251010 00:00:00\"\nSet the clock to a time when both entries are still valid (e.g. CURL_TIME=1728465947, October 2024).\nRun any curl request with --hsts <cache-file> to force curl to load and rewrite the cache.\nInspect the rewritten cache file — it now contains only one entry (the parent). The child entry has been silently dropped.\nAdvance the clock past the parent's expiry but before the child's (e.g. CURL_TIME=1728600000, October 11 2024).\nStart a plaintext HTTP server on localhost and run curl --hsts <cache-file> --resolve child.example.test:80:127.0.0.1 http://child.example.test/.\nObserve that curl sends the request over plaintext HTTP instead of upgrading to HTTPS — the child's still-valid HSTS policy is gone.\nAs a control, repeat with the child listed before the parent in the original cache file — both entries are preserved and the request is correctly upgraded to HTTPS.\nAs a second control, repeat with the child entry without includeSubDomains (no leading dot: child.example.test) — both entries are preserved regardless of ordering.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nUse exact-host lookup for import deduplication, as `Curl_hsts_parse()` already does for network-learned policies. Replace the `hsts_check()` call at line 464 with `subdomain=FALSE`:\n\n```diff\n-    e = hsts_check(h, host, hostlen, subdomain);\n+    e = hsts_check(h, host, hostlen, FALSE);\n```\n\nThis reserves hierarchical matching for deciding whether HSTS applies to a request, while import deduplication only matches on exact hostname.\n\n## Impact\n\n## Summary:\nWhen curl rewrites its HSTS cache after loading, the child entry is gone. After the parent expires, a request to `http://child.example.test` is no longer upgraded to HTTPS. An active network attacker can observe or modify traffic that should have been protected by HSTS.\n\nThe prerequisites limit practical severity: HSTS must be enabled; both entries must use `includeSubDomains`; the parent must be loaded first while still live; the child must outlive it; and the collapsed state must persist until after the parent expires. Reloading from an intact original source after parent expiry restores the child.",
